# OPSEC banlist — public, structural patterns only.
#
# Each non-comment, non-blank line is: <PCRE-style regex>\t<reason>
# (tab-separated, exactly one tab). Backslashes follow Python re semantics
# under `grep -P`. Lines beginning with `#` are comments.
#
# The committed banlist intentionally holds only patterns that have no
# legitimate use in this repository — structural tracker-ID shapes and
# private-network IPv4 ranges. Personal names, internal hostnames, agent
# names, and topic-specific terms live in a local-only file pointed to by
# PIPELOCK_OPSEC_EXTRA (default $HOME/.config/pipelock/opsec-extra.patterns)
# so the public banlist does not itself leak the items we are trying to
# keep out of the repo.
#
# Allowlist: an added line containing the literal substring `OPSEC-OK`
# is exempt from every pattern. Use sparingly and only when the match
# is genuinely intentional (e.g. a test asserting the hook fires).
\b(?:TD|LL)-[0-9]+\b	Internal tech-debt / lessons-learned tracker ID
\bPR-[0-9]{1,3}(?![0-9])	Internal multi-PR sequencing label (use #N for GitHub PRs)
\b10\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\b	Private RFC1918 IPv4 (10.0.0.0/8)
\b192\.168\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\b	Private RFC1918 IPv4 (192.168.0.0/16)
\b172\.(?:1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\b	Private RFC1918 IPv4 (172.16.0.0/12)
